ting to be implemented.\n\nNow looking at releases 05.1 through 08.0, I'm tracking which utilities have been finished and which ones still need work—expand and cut are pending, while unexpand, paste, and most of the file operations like mkdir, cp, and rm are done. The pattern shows some releases fully complete while others have scattered gaps in coverage. Looking at the remaining releases, I can see that Release 11.1 continues with users being implemented, followed by who and pinky. Releases 12.0 through 15.0 show a mix of implemented and not-started items, with Release 13.0 and 14.0 having no implementations at all. The note at the end suggests I should focus on proposing tasks only for items marked as \"ready\" in the requirement states.\n\nNow I'm checking which utilities actually have ready status in the requirements. I see a list of specific requirement IDs across releases 13.0 through 15.0, but I notice there's a discrepancy—many utilities showing as \"not started\" in the code status aren't appearing in the requirement states at all, like format from release 11.0. This means I need to filter my task proposals to only those utilities that have both a \"not started\" status and an explicit \"ready\" designation in the requirements. Looking at the releases with ready requirements, 13.0 is the earliest one I should focus on. For that release, srd106-df has the most ready requirements at 10, while srd107-dir only has R2.4 ready.\n\nI'm realizing I need the actual requirement weights from requirements.yaml to validate the weight budget, but I don't have direct access to that file and I'm not supposed to use tools. The project context only gives me the requirement statuses, not the weights themselves, so I'm stuck trying to figure out how to proceed without that critical information. Just realized the constraints are actually consistent—with default weight 1 per requirement, four requirements max equals a weight total of 4, which aligns perfectly. Now I'm checking which packages need contract tasks before implementation work can begin. The cmd/ packages follow an established pattern with main.go and test files, so the contract is essentially defined by that differential testing approach rather than needing a separate specification task.\n\nLooking at the code status more carefully, all four packages show \"not started,\" but the requirement states for cmd/dir indicate only R2.4 is ready—meaning some work must have already been completed on that package despite the overall status showing not started.\n\nChecking the git log reveals the discrepancy: there are recent commits showing Task 5608 implemented R1.5, R2.1, R2.2, and R2.3 for cmd/dir, with follow-up commits updating requirement states. So cmd/dir actually has code from this generation run, suggesting the code_status data might be outdated or not yet refreshed.\n\nFor srd107-dir, only R2.4 is left to implement—likely a small addition to the existing code. Looking at the package_contracts section, I notice contracts are only defined for pkg/ packages like testutils, sys, and format, not for cmd/ packages. This makes sense since cmd/ packages are main packages without exported types, so the contract pattern doesn't really apply to them the way it does for library packages.\n\nGiven that constraint about not proposing implementation tasks for packages without contracts unless they already exist, and since cmd/ packages follow a standard pattern anyway, I won't propose separate contract tasks for them. Now I need to figure out which tasks to propose—I'm limited to 4 maximum. Release 13.0 has the earliest ready requirements, so I'll prioritize there: srd107-dir with just R2.4 is a quick win since the package already exists, and srd108-vdir with its 10 requirements would be the next candidate despite being a new package. But since vdir is \"equivalent to ls -l -b\", it likely shares a lot with ls/dir.\n3. **srd106-df** - 10 requirements (R3.1-R3.7, R4.1-R4.3). New package.\n4. **srd109-dircolors** - 14 requirements (R1.1-R1.4, R2.1-R2.5, R3.1-R3.5). New package.\n\nWith a max of 4 tasks and max 4 requirements per task:\n\nFor srd107-dir R2.4: 1 task (1 requirement, weight likely 1)\nFor srd108-vdir: 10 requirements → at least 3 tasks (4+4+2)\nFor srd106-df: 10 requirements → at least 3 tasks\nFor srd109-dircolors: 14 requirements → at least 4 tasks\n\nI can only propose 4 tasks total, so I need to prioritize the smallest first, then tackle one of the other SRDs. But I'm realizing I should check dependencies—both dir and vdir rely on ls, which shows as \"not started\" in the code status. Since ls requirements aren't in the requirement_states at all, they might already be complete or handled separately, but if dir and vdir are just wrappers around ls functionality, I need to understand whether ls is actually ready before I can move forward with these. I'm assuming all requirements have equal weight by default. Looking at the release 13.0 tools—df, dir, vdir, and dircolors are all included—so I should propose tasks in canonical order, starting with dir since it already has existing code, then moving to vdir or df. The challenge is that I need to write detailed task descriptions referencing specific SRD files and requirement IDs, but I can't read the files themselves to get the exact details.\n\nI'll structure my proposals to tell the stitch agent to read the SRD files directly. For dir, there's only one requirement left to complete. For vdir, I'll start with the first batch of requirements.\n\nActually, I need to reconsider whether contract tasks are needed for cmd packages. Since these are main packages without exported APIs, they might not require contracts like library packages do. Let me finalize my approach: given the 4-task limit and release 13.0 focus, I'll tackle dir's final requirement first, then move through vdir's requirements in four chunks to complete both utilities. But I need to clarify whether vdir depends on dir or directly on ls before committing to this plan.\n\nLooking at the roadmap, both dir and vdir are wrappers around ls with different output formats, so they likely depend on ls being implemented. The requirement states show these as ready, which suggests either ls is already complete enough or these utilities implement their own logic. Checking the release structure, ls is in release 04.0 while dir and vdir are in 13.0, so I should verify what's actually available before starting.\n\nGiven that srd107-dir already has partial code from a recent commit, I'll prioritize completing that first since it's just R2.4. Then I can use dir as a foundation for vdir since both are thin wrappers around ls with similar logic. I'm also weighing whether to tackle srd106-df or srd109-dircolors instead, noting that df has more requirements to implement. I should reconsider srd106-df since it has fewer remaining requirements to finish. Given the 4-task limit, I could either complete dir and df together, or focus on completing dir and vdir instead—each approach leaves one utility unfinished.\n\nSince dir and vdir are both ls variants, completing dir first gives the stitch agent a solid reference implementation when working on vdir. So I'll prioritize that: finish dir in task 1, then use tasks 2-4 to complete vdir sequentially, with each task building on the previous one.\n\nLet me verify the requirement counts fit the constraint of at most 4 per task. Task 1 has 1 requirement, task 2 has 4, task 3 has 4, and task 4 has 2—all within limits. Now I need to write clear task descriptions that direct the stitch agent to read the SRD and implement each requirement by its specific ID, since I don't have the exact content memorized.\n\nFor the file structure, I'm looking at cmd/dir and cmd/vdir, where dir already exists (so I'd modify main.go and dir_test.go) while vdir is new (so I'd create main.go and vdir_test.go). But the constraint requires splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks, which