rate tasks, and I can't test and implement the same feature in one task. For R2.4, even though it's just one requirement with minimal code changes, I need to follow this strictly. Allocating two full tasks to it seems wasteful, so maybe I should focus all four tasks on either srd106-df or srd108-vdir instead, combining multiple requirements per task while keeping implementation and tests separate. I'm settling on splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks since that's the explicit constraint, even though it conflicts with combining trivial changes. So I'll have Task 0 for srd107-dir R2.4 implementation, Task 1 for its tests, then Tasks 2-3 for srd108-vdir implementation split across requirements, followed by srd108-vdir tests and the rest of the batch. I'll go with splitting impl and tests.\n\nBut hold on - with only 4 tasks available, and wanting to make meaningful progress:\n\nOption A:\n1. srd107-dir R2.4 (impl) \n2. srd107-dir R2.4 (test)\n3. srd108-vdir R1.1-R1.4 (impl)\n4. srd108-vdir R1.5-R1.6, R2.1-R2.2 (impl)\n\nOption B:\n1. srd108-vdir R1.1-R1.4 (impl)\n2. srd108-vdir R1.5-R1.6, R2.1-R2.2 (impl)\n3. srd108-vdir R2.3-R2.4 (impl)\n4. srd108-vdir tests (all requirements)\n\nOption B completes an entire utility in 4 tasks, but I need to check if a single test task covering all 10 requirements fits the constraints. The line count should be fine—differential tests follow a pattern with test cases in a slice literal, so 20-40 test cases across 10 requirements would be around 200-400 lines. However, the constraint explicitly limits each task to at most 4 SRD sub-requirements, which means I'd need to split the tests across multiple tasks anyway.\n\nLet me reconsider what \"at most 4 SRD sub-requirements\" means for test tasks. The constraint is clear about the limit, so I should respect it.\n\nLooking at the numbers: vdir has 10 requirements total, which means I'd need 3 implementation tasks and 3 test tasks just to cover it. With df having 10 remainin